# DB migration hygiene (agent responsibility)

Agents share responsibility for not bloating `scripts/migrations/`. The owner should not have to manually delete piles of one-off scripts.

## Before you add files

1. **Search** `scripts/migrations/` for the same table/feature. If a migration for this change is not yet applied anywhere, **extend that migration** (same `migration_id`) instead of adding a second pair.
2. **One logical change → one `YYYYMMDD_HHMM_slug` pair** (`.up.sql` + `.down.sql`). Put all related DDL for that change in that pair (e.g. several columns on one table in one migration), not three pairs for one feature.
3. **No speculative retries:** If you wrote a migration then changed approach **before** `run_migration.py up` was run on any shared DB, **delete the superseded `.up.sql`/`.down.sql` in the same change** as the replacement. Do not leave orphan drafts.

## New tables (time-series / analytics)

When adding a table keyed by an instant and intended to align with **`historical_data.*_price_history`**, put **`"timestamp" TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE`** (US Eastern wall time, no TZ — same convention as `symbol_data_fetch_pg.convert_utc_to_east_coast`) as the **first** column in `CREATE TABLE`, then business keys (e.g. `end_period_ts`). Avoid storing full raw API blobs in DB columns unless there is a clear retention need.

## After you add files

4. **Same PR / same task:** Complete the full DB protocol (migration + apply where appropriate + schema reference + drift check per `AGENTS.md`). Half-finished migrations left in the tree are debt.
5. **Do not delete** migration pairs that may already be in `system.schema_migrations` on prod (or you break `down` and confuse history). Cleanup of **applied** migrations is **archive/rename policy** only, and only when the user explicitly wants it and understands the runner implications.

## Plan-driven implementation (migrations in scope)

When you are implementing a **plan**, task todos, or any multi-step spec that includes **schema work** (new or changed `scripts/migrations/*.up.sql` / `*.down.sql`, or bringing an existing DB in line with new code that reads new columns):

6. **Actually run local `up` before you call the work done:** After the migration pair is in the repo, run `python3 scripts/db/run_migration.py up <migration_id>` (or `.venv/bin/python scripts/db/run_migration.py up <migration_id>`) against the **local** database this workspace is configured to use (`AGENTS.md` / `get_system_postgresql_connection`). Say in the chat whether apply succeeded or failed (and paste the error if it failed). **Passing unit tests alone does not satisfy this** when tests mock the DB or omit integration paths that hit new catalog.
7. **If local apply is impossible:** Say so explicitly (e.g. no `REC_DB_*` / no Postgres). Do not imply the migration was applied. The operator can run `up` elsewhere; your job is not to silently skip the step.

## Local DB changes → production path (non-negotiable)

8. **No local-only schema drift:** If you change the database on your machine (manual `ALTER` in a GUI, ad-hoc SQL, or relying only on `init_database()` without a migration pair), you must **immediately** fold that change into `scripts/migrations/*.up.sql` / `*.down.sql`, run `python3 scripts/db/run_migration.py up <migration_id>` locally so `system.schema_migrations` matches the repo, and treat **that migration** as what production will run on deploy. **Definition of done:** local DB state is explained by applied migrations in the tree, not by uncommitted one-off DDL.

## Summary

Prefer **fewer, larger, coherent migrations** over many tiny timestamped files. Remove **unapplied** mistakes; never silently remove **applied** history. Plan implementations that require migrations are not complete until **local `run_migration.py up`** has been run or explicitly skipped with reason.
